use console.println
use clock.now

/// What `String.startsWith` costs, at the four size pairs it can be called at.
///
/// ADR 0064 moved the prefix test out of the runtime and into
/// `std.string.startsWith`, a Cove loop over `core.byteLength` and `byteAt`.
/// A migration's own cost is the thing Decision 8 asks a pull request to
/// record, and for this one no whole-program figure can supply it at all:
/// `examples/covefmt` never calls `startsWith`, and `examples/cq` calls it
/// twice, both times while reading its command line. These rows are the
/// operation on its own, and they are the only measurement this migration
/// has.
///
/// # A sibling of `benches/endswith` rather than four more rows in it
///
/// The two operations are the same loop with a different offset, so putting
/// them in one program would have bought one bare-loop control instead of two
/// and made the pair directly comparable inside one process — which is a real
/// argument, and is what is given up here. What it would have cost is a
/// package whose name says `endswith` and whose rows are half about something
/// else, and a doc comment hedging every sentence it now states plainly: the
/// suffix loop's long/short row is cheap because `n - m` is one subtraction,
/// and the prefix loop's is cheap because there is no offset to compute at
/// all. One package per operation also gives `contains` and `indexOf` an
/// obvious place to go when Phase 1 reaches them.
///
/// The sizes are `benches/endswith`'s — five bytes and fifty, against the same
/// eighty-eight-byte receiver — so that a reader who wants the comparison the
/// shared control would have given them can still take it across the two
/// programs, knowing it costs a code layout.
///
/// # The four sizes are four different bodies, not one body at four scales
///
/// The loop compares the **prefix's** bytes, never the receiver's, and it
/// answers on the two lengths alone when the prefix is the longer. So the two
/// dimensions do quite different things and the matrix is what separates them:
///
/// - **short / short** is the ordinary call, five bytes compared;
/// - **short / long** is the refusal on length, and no byte of either string
///   read: the cheapest answer `startsWith` has, and the one a reader would
///   most easily assume costs the same as the others;
/// - **long / short** is the same five bytes compared against a receiver five
///   times as long, which is what says the cost does *not* grow with the
///   receiver — the loop starts at 0 and stops at the prefix's length, and
///   `core.byteLength` is a header load rather than a walk;
/// - **long / long** is fifty bytes compared, which is where the per-byte
///   slope shows up against the per-call constant the other three measure.
///
/// # The loop is in a callee, and that is not a style choice
///
/// The outermost frame is always encoded, so a loop written in `main` is a VM
/// measurement even under `--backend native`, and this benchmark has to be
/// able to speak for both tiers: ADR 0064's whole argument is that the ratio
/// between them flips sign when an intrinsic call becomes Cove. Each row is
/// therefore a call to [`countStartsWith`], whose body is the loop, and `main`
/// does nothing in a turn but read a clock either side of it.
///
/// **That holds only while [`countStartsWith`] is too big to be inlined.**
/// `benches/endswith` records what it looks like when it is not: while
/// `endsWith` was one `IntrinsicCall` its callee was about ten instructions,
/// inside `cove_ir::lower::inline`'s cold `LIMIT` of sixteen, so it folded
/// into [`row`] — which the native tier refuses for the `now()` at its start —
/// and the "native" column was the encoded VM wearing the native tier's name.
/// The Cove `startsWith` is sixteen instructions, which is exactly the cold
/// limit rather than past it — so whether a given site expands it is the
/// caller's frame budget's to decide, not its size's. The site below is inside
/// a `while`, so it is a hot one and `HOT_LIMIT` admits it regardless, and the
/// expansion is what makes [`countStartsWith`] twenty-five instructions:
/// past the limit itself, and so a function the native tier is given. That is
/// checked rather than assumed: `--backend native --boundary` names the
/// compiled and the refused, and the only two refused are [`row`] and `main`,
/// each for the `now()` in it. A later change that makes
/// `startsWith` small again — a bounded run comparison beneath it, say — would
/// put this benchmark back in the state where its native column is the VM,
/// silently. Check the compiled set before believing a native row here.
///
/// [`countBare`] is the control, the same loop with the call taken out, so
/// that what each row reports is the call rather than the turn. **Its branch
/// is always taken**, which matches the three rows that answer `true`; the
/// short/long row answers `false` and so does one addition less per turn than
/// its control does, and is reported cheap by exactly that much. Under
/// `--backend native` that row may read *negative*, and it is not a broken
/// clock: a length refusal there costs less than the addition the control
/// makes, so the difference is below zero and the honest reading of the row
/// is "too cheap to separate from the loop".
///
/// [`turns`] is 200,000 rather than the 2,000,000 `benches/arith` and
/// `benches/builtincall` use, for `benches/endswith`'s reason:
/// `crates/cove-cli/tests/vm_coverage.rs` runs every program in this
/// repository on the tree-walking interpreter as well as on the linear-memory
/// backend, and the long/long row is fifty byte comparisons a turn — 10
/// million of them at this count, and ten times that at the usual one.
fn turns() -> Int {
  200000
}

fn shortText() -> String {
  "crates/main.cove"
}

fn shortPrefix() -> String {
  "crate"
}

/// Eighty-eight bytes, beginning with [`longPrefix`]'s fifty.
fn longText() -> String {
  "crates/cove-runtime/src/vm/intrinsics/text/deeply/nested/directory/tree/module/main.cove"
}

fn longPrefix() -> String {
  "crates/cove-runtime/src/vm/intrinsics/text/deeply/"
}

/// `rounds` prefix tests and nothing else the loop can be about.
///
/// No host call in the body, so that the native tier compiles it and the rows
/// price `startsWith` on the tier that runs it.
fn countStartsWith(text: String, prefix: String, rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = 0
  var i = 0
  while i < rounds {
    if text.startsWith(prefix) {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// [`countStartsWith`]'s loop with the call taken out, and its branch taken
/// every turn.
fn countBare(rounds: Int) -> Int {
  var found = 0
  var i = 0
  while i < rounds {
    if i < rounds {
      found = found + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// How long `rounds` turns of [`countStartsWith`] took, in microseconds, and
/// how many of them answered `true`.
struct Row {
  micros: Int
  found: Int
}

fn row(text: String, prefix: String, rounds: Int) -> Row {
  let from = now()
  let found = countStartsWith(text, prefix, rounds)
  Row(micros: (now() - from).micros(), found: found)
}

export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let rounds = turns()

  let bareFrom = now()
  let bare = countBare(rounds)
  let bareMicros = (now() - bareFrom).micros()

  let shortShort = row(shortText(), shortPrefix(), rounds)
  let shortLong = row(shortText(), longPrefix(), rounds)
  let longShort = row(longText(), shortPrefix(), rounds)
  let longLong = row(longText(), longPrefix(), rounds)

  // What each row answered, checked before any of it is read as a timing: a
  // row that stopped matching would still report a number, and the number
  // would be of something else.
  assertEqual(bare, rounds)?
  assertEqual(shortShort.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(shortLong.found, 0)?
  assertEqual(longShort.found, rounds)?
  assertEqual(longLong.found, rounds)?

  println(
    "bare loop                 {bareMicros * 1000 / rounds} ns/turn ({bare})",
  )?
  println("short/short  (5 bytes)    {(shortShort.micros - bareMicros) * 1000 / rounds} ns/call")?
  println("short/long   (no bytes)   {(shortLong.micros - bareMicros) * 1000 / rounds} ns/call")?
  println("long/short   (5 bytes)    {(longShort.micros - bareMicros) * 1000 / rounds} ns/call")?
  println("long/long    (50 bytes)   {(longLong.micros - bareMicros) * 1000 / rounds} ns/call")?
  Ok(())
}
